WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.492
Our running case is a small outpatient clinic.

00:00:02.492 --> 00:00:05.572
The clinic needs to track patients, providers, appointments, services,

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:08.482
and the services delivered during each appointment.

00:00:08.482 --> 00:00:13.687
Think about which words describe independent things and which words describe relationships or rules.

00:00:13.687 --> 00:00:16.357
Patient, provider, appointment, and service sound like candidate entities.

00:00:16.357 --> 00:00:20.862
A rule such as every appointment must have a provider sounds more like a constraint.

00:00:20.862 --> 00:00:24.262
The design also needs to prevent problems such as duplicate patient identifiers, negative prices,

00:00:24.262 --> 00:00:27.982
impossible appointment times, and service lists stored as comma-separated text.

00:00:27.982 --> 00:00:33.022
Before looking at the schema, try a quick warm-up: which words in this scenario would you initially consider

00:00:33.022 --> 00:00:34.787
tables, and which would you consider business rules?

00:00:34.787 --> 00:00:39.502
Connect this idea to a university system separating students, courses, sections, and enrollments.

00:00:39.502 --> 00:00:41.942
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:41.942 --> 00:00:47.992
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:47.992 --> 00:00:51.142
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:51.142 --> 00:00:55.920
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
